filename   'café notes#1.txt'
naive URI  'file:///home/ada/café notes#1.txt'

WHAT EACH ESCAPING RULE DOES TO IT
   quote(), default safe='/'      /home/ada/caf%C3%A9%20notes%231.txt
   quote() with nothing safe      %2Fhome%2Fada%2Fcaf%C3%A9%20notes%231.txt
   what a terminal usually emits  /home/ada/café%20notes%231.txt

Look at the third row, which is what most tools actually write. The
space and the '#' are percent-encoded, because they would end the URI
or start a fragment. The 'é' is not. It is emitted as its raw UTF-8
bytes, c3 a9, inside something declared to be a URI.

THE SEQUENCE, ASSEMBLED
   \x1b]8;;/home/ada/caf\xe9%20notes%231.txt\x1b\\caf\xe9 notes#1.txt\x1b]8;;\x1b\\

   \x1b]8;;  starts the link and names the target
   \x1b\     is ST, the string terminator
   then the ordinary text the user sees, then an empty OSC 8 to close

   So a clickable filename is ordinary text with an escape sequence
   wrapped round it. Nothing about the text changed -- which is why it
   still measures 16 characters to anything that strips escapes, and
   60 to anything that does not.

WHY THE ACCENT IS THE INTERESTING BYTE
   RFC 3986 says a URI is a sequence of characters from a limited ASCII
   set; anything else is percent-encoded UTF-8. By that rule the third
   row is not a URI at all.
   The OSC 8 specification does not require conformance either -- it
   says the behaviour of non-ASCII bytes in the URI is UNDEFINED. So
   every terminal is free to guess, and they do not all guess the same:
   the link works in the emulator you tested and silently does nothing
   in the next one.

   The safe thing to emit is the second row -- percent-encode
   everything that is not unreserved, including the accent:
   caf%C3%A9%20notes%231.txt
   It is uglier, it is a real URI, and it clicks everywhere.
